{"run_id": "full-r1", "item_id": "reasoning-007", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Friday the 13ths in 2026-2030", "difficulty": "medium", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_off", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning off)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "none", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 10370, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 1930, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 55, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 5120, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "9", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "9"}, "workflow_id": "0sfjdc4e5fe2-d027-11f1-ba7a-56adf5b8ca14", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfjdaaa736e-d027-11f1-ba7a-56adf5b8ca14", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T22:55:15-0500", "answer": "I need to find all Friday the 13ths from January 1, 2026 through December 31, 2030.\n\nLet me check each year by finding what day of the week the 13th falls on for each month.\n\nFirst, I need to know what day of the week January 1, 2026 is. January 1, 2025 is a Wednesday. 2025 is not a leap year (365 days), so January 1, 2026 is a Thursday.\n\n**2026** (starts Thursday, not a leap year):\n- Jan 13: Thursday + 12 days = Tuesday\n- Feb 13: Thursday + 31 + 12 = Thursday + 43 = Thursday + 1 = Friday \u2713 **Friday the 13th**\n- Mar 13: Thursday + 31 + 28 + 12 = Thursday + 71 = Thursday + 1 = Friday \u2713 **Friday the 13th**\n- Apr 13: Thursday + 31 + 28 + 31 + 12 = Thursday + 102 = Thursday + 4 = Monday\n- May 13: Thursday + 31 + 28 + 31 + 30 + 12 = Thursday + 132 = Thursday + 6 = Wednesday\n- Jun 13: Thursday + 31 + 28 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 12 = Thursday + 163 = Thursday + 2 = Saturday\n- Jul 13: Thursday + 31 + 28 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 30 + 12 = Thursday + 193 = Thursday + 4 = Monday\n- Aug 13: Thursday + 31 + 28 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 12 = Thursday + 224 = Thursday + 0 = Thursday\n- Sep 13: Thursday + 31 + 28 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 31 + 12 = Thursday + 255 = Thursday + 3 = Sunday\n- Oct 13: Thursday + 31 + 28 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 31 + 30 + 12 = Thursday + 285 = Thursday + 5 = Tuesday\n- Nov 13: Thursday + 31 + 28 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 12 = Thursday + 316 = Thursday + 1 = Friday \u2713 **Friday the 13th**\n- Dec 13: Thursday + 31 + 28 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 30 + 12 = Thursday + 346 = Thursday + 3 = Sunday\n\n2026: February, March, November = **3 Friday the 13ths**\n\nJanuary 1, 2027: Thursday + 365 = Thursday + 1 = Friday\n\n**2027** (starts Friday, not a leap year):\n- Jan 13: Friday + 12 = Wednesday\n- Feb 13: Friday + 31 + 12 = Friday + 43 = Friday + 1 = Saturday\n- Mar 13: Friday + 31 + 28 + 12 = Friday + 71 = Friday + 1 = Saturday\n- Apr 13: Friday + 102 = Friday + 4 = Tuesday\n- May 13: Friday + 132 = Friday + 6 = Thursday\n- Jun 13: Friday + 163 = Friday + 2 = Sunday\n- Jul 13: Friday + 193 = Friday + 4 = Tuesday\n- Aug 13: Friday + 224 = Friday + 0 = Friday \u2713 **Friday the 13th**\n- Sep 13: Friday + 255 = Friday + 3 = Monday\n- Oct 13: Friday + 285 = Friday + 5 = Wednesday\n- Nov 13: Friday + 316 = Friday + 1 = Saturday\n- Dec 13: Friday + 346 = Friday + 3 = Monday\n\n2027: August = **1 Friday the 13th**\n\nJanuary 1, 2028: Friday + 365 = Friday + 1 = Saturday\n\n**2028** (starts Saturday, leap year):\n- Jan 13: Saturday + 12 = Thursday\n- Feb 13: Saturday + 31 + 12 = Saturday + 43 = Saturday + 1 = Sunday\n- Mar 13: Saturday + 31 + 29 + 12 = Saturday + 72 = Saturday + 2 = Monday\n- Apr 13: Saturday + 31 + 29 + 31 + 12 = Saturday + 103 = Saturday + 5 = Thursday\n- May 13: Saturday + 31 + 29 + 31 + 30 + 12 = Saturday + 133 = Saturday + 0 = Saturday\n- Jun 13: Saturday + 31 + 29 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 12 = Saturday + 164 = Saturday + 3 = Tuesday\n- Jul 13: Saturday + 31 + 29 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 30 + 12 = Saturday + 194 = Saturday + 5 = Thursday\n- Aug 13: Saturday + 31 + 29 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 12 = Saturday + 225 = Saturday + 1 = Sunday\n- Sep 13: Saturday + 31 + 29 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 31 + 12 = Saturday + 256 = Saturday + 4 = Wednesday\n- Oct 13: Saturday + 31 + 29 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 31 + 30 + 12 = Saturday + 286 = Saturday + 6 = Friday \u2713 **Friday the 13th**\n- Nov 13: Saturday + 31 + 29 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 12 = Saturday + 317 = Saturday + 2 = Monday\n- Dec 13: Saturday + 31 + 29 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 30 + 12 = Saturday + 347 = Saturday + 4 = Wednesday\n\n2028: October = **1 Friday the 13th**\n\nJanuary 1, 2029: Saturday + 366 = Saturday + 2 = Monday\n\n**2029** (starts Monday, not a leap year):\n- Jan 13: Monday + 12 = Saturday\n- Feb 13: Monday + 43 = Monday + 1 = Tuesday\n- Mar 13: Monday + 71 = Monday + 1 = Tuesday\n- Apr 13: Monday + 102 = Monday + 4 = Friday \u2713 **Friday the 13th**\n- May 13: Monday + 132 = Monday + 6 = Sunday\n- Jun 13: Monday + 163 = Monday + 2 = Wednesday\n- Jul 13: Monday + 193 = "}
{"run_id": "full-r1", "item_id": "reasoning-005", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Divisible by exactly two of 4, 6 and 9", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l3", "config_label": "Mistral Large 3 (baseline)", "model": "mistral-large-2512", "reasoning_effort": null, "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 12847, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 1514, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 60, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 3939, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "83334", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "83334"}, "workflow_id": "0sfjdb461773-d027-11f1-ba7a-56adf5b8ca14", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfjdaaa4c5c-d027-11f1-ba7a-56adf5b8ca14", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T22:55:16-0500", "answer": "Alright, I have a problem to solve:\n\n**How many integers n with 1 <= n <= 1,000,000 are divisible by exactly two of the three numbers 4, 6, and 9?**\n\nFirst, I need to understand what's being asked. I need to find all integers from 1 to 1,000,000 that are divisible by exactly two out of the three numbers: 4, 6, and 9.\n\n### Understanding the Problem\nI need numbers divisible by exactly two of these three numbers. That means:\n1. Divisible by 4 and 6 but not 9.\n2. Divisible by 4 and 9 but not 6.\n3. Divisible by 6 and 9 but not 4.\n\n### Approach\nI think I need to find the count of numbers divisible by any two numbers but not the third. To do this, I can use the principle of inclusion and exclusion.\n\n### Step 1: Find Numbers Divisible by Pairs\nFirst, I need to find numbers divisible by any two of the numbers.\n\n#### 1. Divisible by 4 and 6\nA number divisible by both 4 and 6 must be divisible by the least common multiple (LCM) of 4 and 6.\n\n- LCM of 4 and 6:\n  - Prime factors of 4: 2^2\n  - Prime factors of 6: 2 * 3\n  - LCM is the highest power of each prime: 2^2 * 3 = 12\n\nSo, numbers divisible by both 4 and 6 are divisible by 12.\n\nBut I need numbers divisible by 4 and 6 but not by 9.\n\n- Total numbers divisible by 12 up to 1,000,000: floor(1,000,000 / 12) = 83,333\n- Numbers divisible by both 12 and 9 must be divisible by LCM(12,9).\n  - LCM of 12 and 9:\n    - 12: 2^2 * 3\n    - 9: 3^2\n    - LCM: 2^2 * 3^2 = 36\n- Total numbers divisible by 36 up to 1,000,000: floor(1,000,000 / 36) \u2248 27,777\n\nTherefore, numbers divisible by 12 but not by 9: 83,333 - 27,777 = 55,556\n\n#### 2. Divisible by 4 and 9\nNow, numbers divisible by both 4 and 9 but not by 6.\n\n- LCM of 4 and 9:\n  - 4: 2^2\n  - 9: 3^2\n  - LCM: 2^2 * 3^2 = 36\n\nTotal numbers divisible by 36 up to 1,000,000: 27,777 (as above)\n\nBut I need numbers divisible by 36 but not by 6. But if a number is divisible by 36, it must be divisible by 6 because 36 is divisible by 6.\n\nTherefore, there are no numbers divisible by both 4 and 9 but not by 6.\n\n#### 3. Divisible by 6 and 9\nNow, numbers divisible by both 6 and 9 but not by 4.\n\n- LCM of 6 and 9:\n  - 6: 2 * 3\n  - 9: 3^2\n  - LCM: 2 * 3^2 = 18\n\nTotal numbers divisible by 18 up to 1,000,000: floor(1,000,000 / 18) \u2248 55,555\n\nNow, numbers divisible by both 18 and 4 must be divisible by LCM(18,4).\n\n- LCM of 18 and 4:\n  - 18: 2 * 3^2\n  - 4: 2^2\n  - LCM: 2^2 * 3^2 = 36\n\nTotal numbers divisible by 36 up to 1,000,000: 27,777 (as above)\n\nTherefore, numbers divisible by 18 but not by 4: 55,555 - 27,777 = 27,778\n\n### Verification\nBut, I need to ensure that I'm not double-counting or missing anything. The problem is about numbers divisible by exactly two of the three numbers.\n\n- **Case 1**: Divisible by 4 and 6 but not 9 \u2192 55,556\n- **Case 2**: Divisible by 4 and 9 but not 6 \u2192 0 (since any number divisible by both 4 and 9 must be divisible by 6)\n- **Case 3**: Divisible by 6 and 9 but not 4 \u2192 27,778\n\n### Total Count\nNow, I need to add the counts from the valid cases:\n- Case 1: 55,556\n- Case 3: 27,778\n\nTotal = 55,556 + 27,778 = 83,334\n\n### Cross-Checking\nJust to be sure, I should think about numbers divisible by all three numbers.\n\n- Numbers divisible by 4, 6, and 9 must be divisible by LCM(4,6,9).\n  - LCM of 4,6,9:\n    - 4: 2^2\n    - 6: 2 * 3\n    - 9: 3^2\n    - LCM: 2^2 * 3^2 = 36\n\nTotal numbers divisible by 36 up to 1,000,000: 27,777.\n\nBut in the previous cases, I already excluded numbers divisible by all three when I subtracted the numbers divisible by the LCM of any two and the third.\n\n### Conclusion\nThe problem is about numbers divisible by exactly two of the three numbers. From the analysis:\n- Only cases 1 and 3 are valid.\n- Case 2 is invalid because any number divisible by both 4 and 9 must be divisible by 6.\n\nTherefore, the total count is 83,334.\n\n### Final Answer\nAfter careful consideration, the number of integers from 1 to 1,000,000 divisible by exactly two of the numbers 4, 6, and 9 is:\n\nANSWER: 83334"}
