{"run_id": "full-r1", "item_id": "reasoning-016", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Four houses: owners and pets", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "sol_high", "config_label": "GPT-6.1 Sol (reasoning high)", "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 8670, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 451, "reasoning_tokens": 379, "input_tokens": 236, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 161, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "Leo, Jon, Ivy, Kim, fish, bird, cat, dog", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "Leo, Jon, Ivy, Kim, fish, bird, cat, dog"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj2a270ae4-d028-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj2790afe9-d028-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T22:57:24-0500", "answer": "| House | Owner | Pet |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Leo | Fish |\n| 2 | Jon | Bird |\n| 3 | Ivy | Cat |\n| 4 | Kim | Dog |\n\nANSWER: Leo, Jon, Ivy, Kim, fish, bird, cat, dog"}
{"run_id": "full-r1", "item_id": "reasoning-014", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Upgrade budget with dependencies", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_off", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning off)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "none", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 9981, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 1912, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 170, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 3923, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "142", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "142"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj2a384914-d028-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj2790afea-d028-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T22:57:25-0500", "answer": "I need to find the optimal combination of upgrades within 100 credits, respecting the constraints.\n\n**Constraints:**\n- U4 requires U3\n- U2 and U8 cannot both be bought\n\n**Let me list the upgrades with cost/benefit:**\n- U1: 28/40\n- U2: 35/52\n- U3: 17/22\n- U4: 40/61 (requires U3)\n- U5: 12/14\n- U6: 23/33\n- U7: 9/10\n- U8: 31/45\n\n**Strategy:** I'll check promising combinations, especially those with high benefit/cost ratios.\n\nBenefit/cost ratios: U7 (1.11), U5 (1.17), U3 (1.29), U6 (1.43), U1 (1.43), U8 (1.45), U2 (1.49), U4 (1.53)\n\nLet me try combinations with U4 (since it has best ratio, but requires U3):\n\n**With U4 + U3:** cost = 57, benefit = 83. Remaining: 43 credits.\n\nWith 43 credits left, best additions:\n- U1 (28) + U7 (9) = 37, benefit 50. Total: 57+37=94, benefit 83+50=133\n- U6 (23) + U5 (12) = 35, benefit 47. Total: 57+35=92, benefit 83+47=130\n- U6 (23) + U7 (9) = 32, benefit 43. Total: 89, benefit 126\n- U1 (28) + U5 (12) = 40, benefit 54. Total: 97, benefit 137\n- U8 (31) + U7 (9) = 40, benefit 55. Total: 97, benefit 138 \u2713 (U8 allowed, U2 not used)\n- U8 (31) + U5 (12) = 43, benefit 59. Total: 100, benefit 142 \u2713\u2713\n\nCheck: U3+U4+U8+U5 = 17+40+31+12 = 100, benefit = 22+61+45+14 = **142**\n\nCan we do better? Try U8+U5+U7 = 31+12+9 = 52, benefit 69. With U3+U4 = 57, total 109 > 100. No.\n\nTry without U4:\n\n**Without U4, with U2 (can't use U8):**\nBest with U2 (35/52). Remaining: 65.\n\nTry U2 + U1 + U6 + U5 + U7 = 35+28+23+12+9 = 107 > 100. Too much.\n\nU2 + U1 + U6 + U7 = 35+28+23+9 = 95, benefit = 52+40+33+10 = 135\nU2 + U1 + U6 + U5 = 35+28+23+12 = 98, benefit = 52+40+33+14 = 139\nU2 + U1 + U3 + U5 + U7 = 35+28+17+12+9 = 101 > 100\nU2 + U6 + U3 + U5 + U7 = 35+23+17+12+9 = 96, benefit = 52+33+22+14+10 = 131\nU2 + U1 + U3 + U7 = 35+28+17+9 = 89, benefit = 52+40+22+10 = 124\nU2 + U1 + U3 + U5 = 35+28+17+12 = 92, benefit = 52+40+22+14 = 128\nU2 + U6 + U1 + U7 = 95, benefit 135 (already checked)\nU2 + U3 + U6 + U5 = 35+17+23+12 = 87, benefit = 52+22+33+14 = 121\n\nTry U2 + U3 + U1 + U6 = 35+17+28+23 = 103 > 100\n\nWhat about U2 + U8? Not allowed.\n\n**Without U4, with U8 (can't use U2):**\nU8 (31/45). Remaining: 69.\n\nU8 + U1 + U6 + U5 + U7 = 31+28+23+12+9 = 103 > 100\nU8 + U1 + U6 + U5 = 31+28+23+12 = 94, benefit = 45+40+33+14 = 132\nU8 + U1 + U6 + U7 = 31+28+23+9 = 91, benefit = 45+40+33+10 = 128\nU8 + U1 + U3 + U5 + U7 = 31+28+17+12+9 = 97, benefit = 45+40+22+14+10 = 131\nU8 + U6 + U3 + U5 + U7 = 31+23+17+12+9 = 92, benefit = 45+33+22+14+10 = 124\nU8 + U1 + U3 + U6 = 31+28+17+23 = 99, benefit = 45+40+22+33 = 140\nU8 + U3 + U1 + U5 + U7 = 97, benefit 131 (checked)\n\nU8 + U1 + U3 + U6 = 99, benefit 140. Can we add U7? 99+9=108 > 100. No.\n\nWhat about U8 + U3 + U6 + U5 + U7 = 92, benefit 124. Add U1? 92+28=120 > 100.\n\nTry U8 + U1 + U5 + U7 + U3 = 31+28+12+9+17 = 97, benefit = 45+40+14+10+22 = 131\n\nBack to **142** with U3+U4+U5+U8. Let me verify: 17+40+12+31 = 100 \u2713, benefit 22+61+14+45 = 142 \u2713\n\nCan we beat 142? Try U3+U4+U6+U7 = 17+40+23+9 = 89, benefit 22+61+33+10 = 126. Add U5? 89+12=101 > 100.\n\nU3+U4+U1+U7 = 17+40+28+9 = 94, benefit 22+61+40+10 = 133. Add U5? 94+12=106 > 100.\n\nU3+U4+U6+U5 = 17+40+23+12 = 92, benefit 22+61+33+14 = 130. Add U7? 92+9=101 > 100.\n\nU3+U4+U1+U5 = 17+40+28+12 = 97, benefit 22+61+40+14 = 137. Add U7? 97+9=106 > 100.\n\nU3+U4+U8+U7 = 17+40+31+9 = 97, benefit 22+61+45+10 = 138. Add U5? 97+12=109 > 100.\n\nSo U3+U4+U5+U8 = 100, benefit 142 seems best with U4.\n\nWithout U4, best was 140 (U8+U1+U3+U6).\n\nLet me check if there's a combination with U2 that beats 142:\nU2+U1+U6+U5 = 98, benefit 139. Add U7? 98+9=107 > 100.\n\nU2+U3+U6+U5+U7 = 96, benefit 131.\n\nWhat about U2+U1+U3+U5+U7? = 35+28+17+12+9 = 101 > 100.\n\nU2+U6+U3+U5 = 87, benefit 121. Add U7? 96, benefit 131. Add U1? 115 > 100.\n\nTry U1+U2+U3+U6 = 35+28+17+23 = 103 > 100.\n\nU1+U2+U5+U7+U3 = 35+28+12+9+17 = 101 > 100.\n\nU1+U2+U6+U7 = 95, benefit 135. Add U5? 107 > 100.\n\nSo 142 appears to be the maximum.\n\nANSWER: 142"}
{"run_id": "full-r1", "item_id": "reasoning-016", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Four houses: owners and pets", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_off", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning off)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "none", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 10435, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 1575, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 237, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 4535, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "Leo, Jon, Ivy, Kim, fish, bird, cat, dog", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "Leo, Jon, Ivy, Kim, Fish, Bird, Cat, Dog"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj2a270ae3-d028-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj2790afe9-d028-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T22:57:25-0500", "answer": "I need to solve this step-by-step.\n\nLet me start by listing the constraints:\n1. Ivy and Kim live next door to each other\n2. Jon lives immediately to the left of the blue house\n3. Red house is somewhere to the right of Leo's house\n4. Leo does not live in the green house\n5. Kim lives at least two houses away from the green house\n6. Dog lives at least two houses away from Jon\n7. Fish lives immediately to the left of Jon's house\n8. Bird lives next door to Ivy\n\nFrom constraint 7: Fish is immediately left of Jon. So Jon can't be in house 1. Possible: (Fish, Jon) in positions (1,2), (2,3), or (3,4).\n\nFrom constraint 2: Jon is immediately left of blue house. So (Jon, Blue) in positions (1,2), (2,3), or (3,4).\n\nCombining 2 and 7: Fish is left of Jon, and Jon is left of Blue. So we have Fish-Jon-Blue in three consecutive houses. This means positions (1,2,3) or (2,3,4).\n\nCase A: Fish in 1, Jon in 2, Blue in 3\nCase B: Fish in 2, Jon in 3, Blue in 4\n\n**Case A: Fish=1, Jon=2, Blue=3**\n\nFrom constraint 6: Dog is at least 2 houses away from Jon (house 2). So Dog is in house 4 (distance 2) or house... wait, house 1 is distance 1, house 3 is distance 1, house 4 is distance 2. So Dog must be in house 4.\n\nSo pets: 1=Fish, 2=?, 3=?, 4=Dog. Remaining pets: cat, bird for houses 2,3.\n\nFrom constraint 8: Bird is next door to Ivy.\n\nFrom constraint 1: Ivy and Kim are next door to each other.\n\nFrom constraint 5: Kim is at least 2 houses away from green house.\n\nFrom constraint 3: Red is to the right of Leo's house.\n\nFrom constraint 4: Leo is not in green house.\n\nColors: Blue is in house 3. Remaining: red, green, white for houses 1,2,4.\n\nLet me try owners. Jon is in house 2. Remaining owners: Ivy, Kim, Leo for houses 1,3,4.\n\nIvy and Kim must be adjacent. Possible pairs: (1,3) no, (3,4) yes, (1,?) - house 1 and 2 but 2 is Jon. So Ivy-Kim must be in (3,4) or (1,?) - actually (1,2) no, (2,3) no. So only (3,4) works for Ivy-Kim adjacency, meaning Leo is in house 1.\n\nSo: Leo=1, Jon=2, and {Ivy,Kim}={3,4}.\n\nFrom constraint 3: Red is to the right of Leo (house 1). So Red is in 2, 3, or 4. But house 3 is Blue. So Red is in 2 or 4.\n\nFrom constraint 4: Leo (house 1) is not in green. So house 1 is red, white, or... wait, house 3 is blue. House 1 could be red, green, or white, but not green. So house 1 is red or white.\n\nIf house 1 is red: But red must be to the right of Leo. Leo is in house 1, so red must be in 2,3,4. House 1 can't be red. So house 1 is white.\n\nSo house 1 = white. Remaining colors: red, green for houses 2,4 (since 3=blue).\n\nRed is in 2 or 4. Green is in the other.\n\nFrom constraint 5: Kim is at least 2 houses away from green.\n\nIf green is in house 2: Kim must be in house 4 (distance 2) or house... house 1 is distance 1, house 3 is distance 1, house 4 is distance 2. So Kim=4, Ivy=3.\n\nIf green is in house 4: Kim must be in house 1 or 2. But house 1 is Leo, house 2 is Jon. So Kim can't be placed. Contradiction.\n\nSo green is in house 2, red is in house 4. Kim=4, Ivy=3.\n\nCheck: Kim (house 4) and green (house 2): distance is 2. \u2713\n\nSo owners: 1=Leo, 2=Jon, 3=Ivy, 4=Kim.\n\nPets: 1=Fish, 4=Dog. Houses 2,3 have cat and bird.\n\nFrom constraint 8: Bird is next door to Ivy (house 3). So bird is in house 2 or 4. House 4 is Dog. So bird is in house 2. Thus cat is in house 3.\n\nPets: 1=Fish, 2=Bird, 3=Cat, 4=Dog.\n\nLet me verify all constraints:\n1. Ivy(3) and Kim(4) next door? Yes \u2713\n2. Jon(2) immediately left of blue(3)? Yes \u2713\n3. Red(4) to the right of Leo(1)? Yes \u2713\n4. Leo(1) not in green(2)? Yes, Leo is in white \u2713\n5. Kim(4) at least 2 away from green(2)? Distance is 2 \u2713\n6. Dog(4) at least 2 away from Jon(2)? Distance is 2 \u2713\n7. Fish(1) immediately left of Jon(2)? Yes \u2713\n8. Bird(2) next door to Ivy(3)? Yes \u2713\n\nAll constraints satisfied!\n\nLet me also check Case B quickly to make sure there's no other solution.\n\n**Case B: Fish=2, Jon=3, Blue=4**\n\nFrom constraint 6: Dog at least 2 away from Jon(3). So Dog in house 1 (distance 2). House 2 is distance 1, house 4 is distance 1. So"}
{"run_id": "full-r1", "item_id": "reasoning-012", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Counting paths in a dependency graph", "difficulty": "medium", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l3", "config_label": "Mistral Large 3 (baseline)", "model": "mistral-large-2512", "reasoning_effort": null, "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 10473, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 1218, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 135, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 3151, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "26", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "26"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj2a167096-d028-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj279061c4-d028-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T22:57:25-0500", "answer": "Alright, let's tackle this problem step by step. We have a directed acyclic graph (DAG) representing a build pipeline, where each edge indicates that one step can be followed by another. Our goal is to find out how many different directed paths exist from the start node `S` to the terminal node `T`.\n\n### Understanding the Graph\n\nFirst, let's list out all the edges to visualize the graph better:\n\n1. S \u2192 A\n2. S \u2192 B\n3. S \u2192 C\n4. A \u2192 D\n5. A \u2192 E\n6. B \u2192 D\n7. B \u2192 E\n8. B \u2192 F\n9. C \u2192 E\n10. C \u2192 F\n11. D \u2192 G\n12. D \u2192 T\n13. E \u2192 G\n14. E \u2192 H\n15. F \u2192 H\n16. F \u2192 T\n17. G \u2192 T\n18. H \u2192 T\n19. A \u2192 B\n20. C \u2192 B\n\nNow, let's identify all the nodes and their connections:\n\n- **S**: A, B, C\n- **A**: D, E, B\n- **B**: D, E, F\n- **C**: E, F, B\n- **D**: G, T\n- **E**: G, H\n- **F**: H, T\n- **G**: T\n- **H**: T\n- **T**: (no outgoing edges)\n\n### Approach to Count Paths\n\nTo count the number of paths from `S` to `T`, we can use dynamic programming or memoization. The idea is to compute the number of ways to reach `T` from each node by summing the number of ways from its successors.\n\nWe'll define `paths(node)` as the number of distinct paths from `node` to `T`. Then:\n\n- `paths(T) = 1` (there's one way to be at T: do nothing)\n- For any other node `X`, `paths(X) = sum(paths(Y) for all Y such that X \u2192 Y)`\n\nWe'll compute `paths(S)` by working backwards from `T`.\n\n### Computing Paths for Each Node\n\nLet's compute `paths` for each node starting from `T` and moving backwards.\n\n1. **T**:\n   - `paths(T) = 1`\n\n2. **G**:\n   - G \u2192 T\n   - `paths(G) = paths(T) = 1`\n\n3. **H**:\n   - H \u2192 T\n   - `paths(H) = paths(T) = 1`\n\n4. **D**:\n   - D \u2192 G, D \u2192 T\n   - `paths(D) = paths(G) + paths(T) = 1 + 1 = 2`\n\n5. **E**:\n   - E \u2192 G, E \u2192 H\n   - `paths(E) = paths(G) + paths(H) = 1 + 1 = 2`\n\n6. **F**:\n   - F \u2192 H, F \u2192 T\n   - `paths(F) = paths(H) + paths(T) = 1 + 1 = 2`\n\n7. **A**:\n   - A \u2192 D, A \u2192 E, A \u2192 B\n   - `paths(A) = paths(D) + paths(E) + paths(B)`\n   - We don't know `paths(B)` yet, so we'll come back to this.\n\n8. **B**:\n   - B \u2192 D, B \u2192 E, B \u2192 F\n   - `paths(B) = paths(D) + paths(E) + paths(F) = 2 + 2 + 2 = 6`\n\n   Now, let's revisit `A`:\n   - `paths(A) = paths(D) + paths(E) + paths(B) = 2 + 2 + 6 = 10`\n\n9. **C**:\n   - C \u2192 E, C \u2192 F, C \u2192 B\n   - `paths(C) = paths(E) + paths(F) + paths(B) = 2 + 2 + 6 = 10`\n\n10. **S**:\n    - S \u2192 A, S \u2192 B, S \u2192 C\n    - `paths(S) = paths(A) + paths(B) + paths(C) = 10 + 6 + 10 = 26`\n\n### Verifying the Calculations\n\nLet me double-check the calculations to ensure no mistakes were made.\n\n- `paths(T) = 1`\n- `paths(G) = 1`\n- `paths(H) = 1`\n- `paths(D) = paths(G) + paths(T) = 1 + 1 = 2`\n- `paths(E) = paths(G) + paths(H) = 1 + 1 = 2`\n- `paths(F) = paths(H) + paths(T) = 1 + 1 = 2`\n- `paths(B) = paths(D) + paths(E) + paths(F) = 2 + 2 + 2 = 6`\n- `paths(A) = paths(D) + paths(E) + paths(B) = 2 + 2 + 6 = 10`\n- `paths(C) = paths(E) + paths(F) + paths(B) = 2 + 2 + 6 = 10`\n- `paths(S) = paths(A) + paths(B) + paths(C) = 10 + 6 + 10 = 26`\n\nEverything checks out!\n\n### Final Answer\n\nAfter carefully computing the number of paths from each node to `T` and summing them up appropriately, we find that there are **26** different directed paths from `S` to `T`.\n\nANSWER: 26"}
{"run_id": "full-r1", "item_id": "reasoning-014", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Upgrade budget with dependencies", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "sol_low", "config_label": "GPT-6.1 Sol (reasoning low)", "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "reasoning_effort": "low", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 11040, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 526, "reasoning_tokens": 340, "input_tokens": 171, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 569, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "142", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "142"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj2a382203-d028-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj2790afea-d028-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T22:57:26-0500", "answer": "The largest total benefit is **142**, achieved by buying **U3, U4, U5, and U8**:\n\n- Cost: \\(17+40+12+31=100\\)\n- Benefit: \\(22+61+14+45=142\\)\n\nThis satisfies both rules: U3 is included with U4, and U2 is not included with U8.\n\nFor optimality, checking the feasible subsets in each case gives:\n\n| Case | Maximum benefit |\n|---|---:|\n| U4 included (so U3 is required) | 142 |\n| U4 excluded, U2 included | 139 |\n| U4 excluded, U8 included | 140 |\n| U4, U2, and U8 all excluded | 119 |\n\nThese cases cover every valid selection, so no higher benefit is possible.\n\nANSWER: 142"}
{"run_id": "full-r1", "item_id": "reasoning-012", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Counting paths in a dependency graph", "difficulty": "medium", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_high", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning high)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 11150, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 1717, "reasoning_tokens": 1539, "input_tokens": 134, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 331, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 3932, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "26", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "26"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj2a167092-d028-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj279061c4-d028-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T22:57:26-0500", "answer": "Let \\(P(x)\\) be the number of directed paths from node \\(x\\) to \\(T\\).\n\n- \\(P(T)=1\\)\n- \\(P(G)=1\\), \\(P(H)=1\\)\n- \\(P(D)=P(G)+P(T)=2\\)\n- \\(P(E)=P(G)+P(H)=2\\)\n- \\(P(F)=P(H)+P(T)=2\\)\n- \\(P(B)=P(D)+P(E)+P(F)=2+2+2=6\\)\n- \\(P(A)=P(D)+P(E)+P(B)=2+2+6=10\\)\n- \\(P(C)=P(E)+P(F)+P(B)=2+2+6=10\\)\n- \\(P(S)=P(A)+P(B)+P(C)=10+6+10=26\\)\n\nANSWER: 26"}
{"run_id": "full-r1", "item_id": "reasoning-010", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Shift-and-xor hash trace", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "sol_high", "config_label": "GPT-6.1 Sol (reasoning high)", "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 11148, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 806, "reasoning_tokens": 512, "input_tokens": 102, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 560, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "45578", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "45578"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj2a4b5bf9-d028-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj279061c5-d028-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T22:57:27-0500", "answer": "Each iteration uses the previous `x` for both shifts, then keeps only the lowest 16 bits with `& 0xFFFF`.\n\n| `i` | `ch` | `ord(ch) * (i + 1)` | New `x` (hex) | New `x` (decimal) |\n|---:|:---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 0 | `o` | 111 | `0x006F` | 111 |\n| 1 | `r` | 228 | `0x0D09` | 3337 |\n| 2 | `k` | 321 | `0xA1C0` | 41408 |\n| 3 | `e` | 404 | `0x2DAC` | 11692 |\n| 4 | `s` | 575 | `0xB20A` | 45578 |\n\nFor example, the final iteration is:\n```text\n((0x2DAC << 5) ^ (0x2DAC >> 3) ^ 0x023F) & 0xFFFF\n= (0x5B580 ^ 0x05B5 ^ 0x023F) & 0xFFFF\n= 0xB20A\n= 45578\n```\n\nANSWER: 45578"}
{"run_id": "full-r1", "item_id": "reasoning-009", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Round-robin scheduler with arrivals", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l3", "config_label": "Mistral Large 3 (baseline)", "model": "mistral-large-2512", "reasoning_effort": null, "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 14526, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 1612, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 256, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 3446, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "b:9,d:11,a:16,c:18,e:20", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "b:9,d:11,a:16,c:18,e:20"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj2a093ad2-d028-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj279088d8-d028-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T22:57:30-0500", "answer": "Let's trace this program step by step. This is a simulation of a round-robin scheduling algorithm with a time quantum of 2.\n\nInitial state:\n- arrivals: deque([(0, \"a\", 5), (1, \"b\", 3), (2, \"c\", 6), (6, \"d\", 2), (7, \"e\", 4)])\n- quantum: 2\n- clock: 0\n- ready: deque()\n- done: []\n\nFirst `admit()` call:\n- arrivals[0][0] (0) <= clock (0): admit \"a\" (need=5)\n- arrivals[0][0] (1) <= clock (0): no, stop\n- ready: [[\"a\", 5]]\n\nFirst iteration of while loop:\n- ready is not empty\n- job = [\"a\", 5]\n- run = min(2, 5) = 2\n- clock += 2 \u2192 clock = 2\n- job[1] -= 2 \u2192 job = [\"a\", 3]\n- admit():\n  - arrivals[0][0] (1) <= clock (2): admit \"b\" (need=3)\n  - arrivals[0][0] (2) <= clock (2): admit \"c\" (need=6)\n  - arrivals[0][0] (6) <= clock (2): no, stop\n  - ready: [[\"a\", 3]] (after pop) \u2192 becomes [[\"a\", 3], [\"b\", 3], [\"c\", 6]] after admit\n- job[1] (3) > 0 \u2192 ready.append([\"a\", 3])\n- ready: [[\"b\", 3], [\"c\", 6], [\"a\", 3]]\n\nSecond iteration:\n- job = [\"b\", 3]\n- run = min(2, 3) = 2\n- clock += 2 \u2192 clock = 4\n- job[1] -= 2 \u2192 job = [\"b\", 1]\n- admit():\n  - arrivals[0][0] (6) <= clock (4): no, stop\n- job[1] (1) > 0 \u2192 ready.append([\"b\", 1])\n- ready: [[\"c\", 6], [\"a\", 3], [\"b\", 1]]\n\nThird iteration:\n- job = [\"c\", 6]\n- run = min(2, 6) = 2\n- clock += 2 \u2192 clock = 6\n- job[1] -= 2 \u2192 job = [\"c\", 4]\n- admit():\n  - arrivals[0][0] (6) <= clock (6): admit \"d\" (need=2)\n  - arrivals[0][0] (7) <= clock (6): no, stop\n  - ready: [[\"a\", 3], [\"b\", 1], [\"c\", 4], [\"d\", 2]]\n- job[1] (4) > 0 \u2192 ready.append([\"c\", 4])\n- ready: [[\"a\", 3], [\"b\", 1], [\"d\", 2], [\"c\", 4]]\n\nFourth iteration:\n- job = [\"a\", 3]\n- run = min(2, 3) = 2\n- clock += 2 \u2192 clock = 8\n- job[1] -= 2 \u2192 job = [\"a\", 1]\n- admit():\n  - arrivals[0][0] (7) <= clock (8): admit \"e\" (need=4)\n  - ready: [[\"b\", 1], [\"d\", 2], [\"c\", 4], [\"a\", 1], [\"e\", 4]]\n- job[1] (1) > 0 \u2192 ready.append([\"a\", 1])\n- ready: [[\"b\", 1], [\"d\", 2], [\"c\", 4], [\"e\", 4], [\"a\", 1]]\n\nFifth iteration:\n- job = [\"b\", 1]\n- run = min(2, 1) = 1\n- clock += 1 \u2192 clock = 9\n- job[1] -= 1 \u2192 job = [\"b\", 0]\n- admit(): no new arrivals\n- job[1] (0) \u2192 done.append(\"b:9\")\n- ready: [[\"d\", 2], [\"c\", 4], [\"e\", 4], [\"a\", 1]]\n\nSixth iteration:\n- job = [\"d\", 2]\n- run = min(2, 2) = 2\n- clock += 2 \u2192 clock = 11\n- job[1] -= 2 \u2192 job = [\"d\", 0]\n- admit(): no new arrivals\n- job[1] (0) \u2192 done.append(\"d:11\")\n- ready: [[\"c\", 4], [\"e\", 4], [\"a\", 1]]\n\nSeventh iteration:\n- job = [\"c\", 4]\n- run = min(2, 4) = 2\n- clock += 2 \u2192 clock = 13\n- job[1] -= 2 \u2192 job = [\"c\", 2]\n- admit(): no new arrivals\n- job[1] (2) > 0 \u2192 ready.append([\"c\", 2])\n- ready: [[\"e\", 4], [\"a\", 1], [\"c\", 2]]\n\nEighth iteration:\n- job = [\"e\", 4]\n- run = min(2, 4) = 2\n- clock += 2 \u2192 clock = 15\n- job[1] -= 2 \u2192 job = [\"e\", 2]\n- admit(): no new arrivals\n- job[1] (2) > 0 \u2192 ready.append([\"e\", 2])\n- ready: [[\"a\", 1], [\"c\", 2], [\"e\", 2]]\n\nNinth iteration:\n- job = [\"a\", 1]\n- run = min(2, 1) = 1\n- clock += 1 \u2192 clock = 16\n- job[1] -= 1 \u2192 job = [\"a\", 0]\n- admit(): no new arrivals\n- job[1] (0) \u2192 done.append(\"a:16\")\n- ready: [[\"c\", 2], [\"e\", 2]]\n\nTenth iteration:\n- job = [\"c\", 2]\n- run = min(2, 2) = 2\n- clock += 2 \u2192 clock = 18\n- job[1] -= 2 \u2192 job = [\"c\", 0]\n- admit(): no new arrivals\n- job[1] (0) \u2192 done.append(\"c:18\")\n- ready: [[\"e\", 2]]\n\nEleventh iteration:\n- job = [\"e\", 2]\n- run = min(2, 2) = 2\n- clock += 2 \u2192 clock = 20\n- job[1] -= 2 \u2192 job = [\"e\", 0]\n- admit(): no new arrivals\n- job[1] (0) \u2192 done.append(\"e:20\")\n- ready: []\n\nFinal `done` list: [\"b:9\", \"d:11\", \"a:16\", \"c:18\", \"e:20\"]\n\nANSWER: b:9,d:11,a:16,c:18,e:20"}
{"run_id": "full-r1", "item_id": "reasoning-014", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Upgrade budget with dependencies", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "sol_high", "config_label": "GPT-6.1 Sol (reasoning high)", "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 15860, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 950, "reasoning_tokens": 762, "input_tokens": 171, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 531, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "142", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "142"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj2a387026-d028-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj2790afea-d028-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T22:57:31-0500", "answer": "The maximum total benefit is **142**, achieved by buying **U3, U4, U5, and U8**:\n\n- Cost: \\(17 + 40 + 12 + 31 = 100\\)\n- Benefit: \\(22 + 61 + 14 + 45 = 142\\)\n\nThis satisfies both rules: U3 is included with U4, and U2 is not bought alongside U8.\n\nTo verify optimality, checking the feasible subsets in these exhaustive cases gives:\n\n| Case | Highest benefit |\n|---|---:|\n| U4 included (so U3 is required) | 142 |\n| U4 excluded, U2 included | 139 |\n| U4 excluded, U8 included | 140 |\n| U4, U2, and U8 all excluded | 119 |\n\nANSWER: 142"}
{"run_id": "full-r1", "item_id": "reasoning-011", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Shortest route that must pass through D", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_high", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning high)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 19069, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 3290, "reasoning_tokens": 3173, "input_tokens": 163, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 249, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 8636, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "13", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "13"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj28fef69a-d028-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj279061c6-d028-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T22:57:32-0500", "answer": "The route must include D, so split it into A\u2192D and D\u2192H.\n\n- Best A\u2192D route: A\u2192C\u2192D = 1 + 5 = 6\n- Best D\u2192H route: D\u2192E\u2192F\u2192G\u2192H = 1 + 2 + 1 + 3 = 7\n\nCombined route: A\u2192C\u2192D\u2192E\u2192F\u2192G\u2192H  \nTotal time: 6 + 7 = 13 minutes, with no location visited twice.\n\nANSWER: 13"}
{"run_id": "full-r1", "item_id": "reasoning-011", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Shortest route that must pass through D", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l3", "config_label": "Mistral Large 3 (baseline)", "model": "mistral-large-2512", "reasoning_effort": null, "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 20901, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 2281, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 165, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 6447, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "13", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "13"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj28ff1dad-d028-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj279061c6-d028-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T22:57:34-0500", "answer": "Alright, let's tackle this problem step by step. We need to find the minimum total travel time from point A to point H in a one-way road network, with the condition that the route must pass through D and no location can be visited more than once.\n\n### Understanding the Problem\n\nFirst, let's list out all the roads with their travel times for clarity:\n\n- A -> B: 4\n- A -> C: 1\n- A -> D: 7\n- B -> E: 3\n- B -> G: 10\n- C -> B: 2\n- C -> D: 5\n- C -> F: 9\n- D -> E: 1\n- D -> G: 6\n- E -> F: 2\n- E -> H: 12\n- F -> G: 1\n- F -> H: 8\n- G -> H: 3\n\nOur goal is to find the shortest path from A to H that goes through D exactly once (since visiting no location twice implies we can't go through D more than once), and no other location is repeated.\n\n### Approach\n\nTo find the shortest path from A to H passing through D without revisiting any location, we can break the problem into two parts:\n\n1. Find the shortest path from A to D without revisiting any locations.\n2. Find the shortest path from D to H without revisiting any locations (and ensuring that none of the locations from the A to D path are reused, except possibly D itself, but since we can't revisit, D is only at the junction).\n\nHowever, since the entire path must not revisit any location, the two paths (A to D and D to H) must not share any common locations except D.\n\nTherefore, the strategy is:\n\n- Enumerate all possible paths from A to D without revisiting any locations.\n- For each such path, enumerate all possible paths from D to H that do not revisit any locations already in the A to D path (except D).\n- Calculate the total time for each combined path and keep track of the minimum.\n\nGiven the size of the network, this is manageable manually.\n\n### Finding All Paths from A to D Without Revisiting Locations\n\nLet's list all possible paths from A to D where no location is visited more than once.\n\nStarting from A, possible first steps:\n\n1. A -> B\n   - From B: B -> E, B -> G\n     - B -> E: E -> F, E -> H\n       - E -> F: F -> G, F -> H\n         - F -> G: G -> H\n           - Path: A -> B -> E -> F -> G -> H (but we're going to D, so not relevant yet)\n         - F -> H: ends at H, not D\n       - E -> H: ends at H, not D\n     - B -> G: G -> H\n       - Path: A -> B -> G -> H (not to D)\n   - Doesn't seem to reach D directly from B. Maybe longer paths:\n     - A -> B -> E -> F -> D? But F doesn't go to D.\n     - A -> B -> E -> D? E doesn't go to D.\n     - A -> B -> C? But C is not reachable from B in the given roads.\n   - So, no paths from A -> B to D without revisiting.\n\nWait, looking back, from B, the roads are B -> E and B -> G. Neither E nor G leads to D directly. So no paths from A -> B to D.\n\n2. A -> C\n   - From C: C -> B, C -> D, C -> F\n     - C -> B: B -> E, B -> G\n       - B -> E: E -> F, E -> H\n         - E -> F: F -> G, F -> H\n           - F -> G: G -> H\n           - F -> H: ends\n         - E -> H: ends\n       - B -> G: G -> H\n     - Doesn't reach D from C -> B.\n     - C -> D: direct to D\n       - Path: A -> C -> D (length: 1 + 5 = 6)\n     - C -> F: F -> G, F -> H\n       - F -> G: G -> H\n       - F -> H: ends\n     - Doesn't reach D from C -> F.\n   - So, one path: A -> C -> D (6)\n\n3. A -> D\n   - Direct path: A -> D (7)\n\nSo, the possible paths from A to D without revisiting are:\n- A -> C -> D: 6\n- A -> D: 7\n\n### Finding Paths from D to H Without Revisiting Locations in A to D Path\n\nNow, for each A to D path, we'll find D to H paths that don't revisit any locations in the A to D path (except D).\n\n**Path 1: A -> C -> D (locations: A, C, D)**\n\nFrom D, possible next steps without revisiting A, C:\n- D -> E\n- D -> G\n\nLet's explore both:\n\na. D -> E\n   - From E: E -> F, E -> H\n     - E -> F: F -> G, F -> H\n       - F -> G: G -> H\n         - Path: D -> E -> F -> G -> H (length: 1 + 2 + 1 + 3 = 7)\n       - F -> H: D -> E -> F -> H (length: 1 + 2 + 8 = 11)\n     - E -> H: D -> E -> H (length: 1 + 12 = 13)\n   - Possible paths:\n     - D -> E -> F -> G -> H: 7\n     - D -> E -> F -> H: 11\n     - D -> E -> H: 1"}
{"run_id": "full-r1", "item_id": "reasoning-010", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Shift-and-xor hash trace", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l3", "config_label": "Mistral Large 3 (baseline)", "model": "mistral-large-2512", "reasoning_effort": null, "pass": false, "score": 0.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 19457, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 2147, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 101, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 3400, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 0.0, "pass": false, "expected": "45578", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "49574"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj2a4b5bf8-d028-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj279061c5-d028-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T22:57:35-0500", "answer": "Let's trace this program step by step by hand.\n\nWe start with `x = 0` and iterate over the string `\"orkes\"` with `enumerate`, which gives us pairs of `(index, character)`.\n\nThe string `\"orkes\"` has characters and indices:\n- index 0: 'o'\n- index 1: 'r'\n- index 2: 'k'\n- index 3: 'e'\n- index 4: 's'\n\nThe formula inside the loop is:\n```python\nx = ((x << 5) ^ (x >> 3) ^ (ord(ch) * (i + 1))) & 0xFFFF\n```\n\nWe'll compute `x` for each iteration.\n\n---\n\n**Iteration 0:**\n- `i = 0`, `ch = 'o'`\n- `ord('o')` is 111\n- `x = ((0 << 5) ^ (0 >> 3) ^ (111 * (0 + 1))) & 0xFFFF`\n- `0 << 5` is 0\n- `0 >> 3` is 0\n- `111 * 1` is 111\n- `0 ^ 0 ^ 111` is 111\n- `111 & 0xFFFF` is 111\n- `x = 111`\n\n---\n\n**Iteration 1:**\n- `i = 1`, `ch = 'r'`\n- `ord('r')` is 114\n- `x = ((111 << 5) ^ (111 >> 3) ^ (114 * (1 + 1))) & 0xFFFF`\n- `111 << 5` is `111 * 32 = 3552`\n- `111 >> 3` is `111 // 8 = 13` (integer division)\n- `114 * 2 = 228`\n- `3552 ^ 13 ^ 228`\n  - `3552 ^ 13`:\n    - 3552 in binary: `110111010000`\n    - 13 in binary:    `000000001101`\n    - XOR:            `110111011101` = 3549\n  - `3549 ^ 228`:\n    - 3549 in binary: `110111011101`\n    - 228 in binary:  `000011100100`\n    - XOR:            `110100111001` = 3385\n- `3385 & 0xFFFF` is 3385\n- `x = 3385`\n\n---\n\n**Iteration 2:**\n- `i = 2`, `ch = 'k'`\n- `ord('k')` is 107\n- `x = ((3385 << 5) ^ (3385 >> 3) ^ (107 * (2 + 1))) & 0xFFFF`\n- `3385 << 5` is `3385 * 32 = 108320`\n- `3385 >> 3` is `3385 // 8 = 423`\n- `107 * 3 = 321`\n- `108320 ^ 423 ^ 321`\n  - `108320 ^ 423`:\n    - 108320 in binary: `11010010100000000`\n    - 423 in binary:     `000000110100111`\n    - XOR:              `11010001110011111` = 107231\n  - `107231 ^ 321`:\n    - 107231 in binary: `11010001110011111`\n    - 321 in binary:    `000000101000001`\n    - XOR:              `11010000010011110` = 106526\n- `106526 & 0xFFFF` is `106526 - 65536 = 40990` (since 0xFFFF is 65535, and 106526 > 65535, we take modulo 65536)\n  - `106526 % 65536 = 40990`\n- `x = 40990`\n\n---\n\n**Iteration 3:**\n- `i = 3`, `ch = 'e'`\n- `ord('e')` is 101\n- `x = ((40990 << 5) ^ (40990 >> 3) ^ (101 * (3 + 1))) & 0xFFFF`\n- `40990 << 5` is `40990 * 32 = 1311680`\n- `40990 >> 3` is `40990 // 8 = 5123`\n- `101 * 4 = 404`\n- `1311680 ^ 5123 ^ 404`\n  - `1311680 ^ 5123`:\n    - 1311680 in binary: `101000000011110000000`\n    - 5123 in binary:     `00000001010000000011`\n    - XOR:               `10100001011110000011` = 1316739\n  - `1316739 ^ 404`:\n    - 1316739 in binary: `101000010111100000011`\n    - 404 in binary:      `00000000000110010100`\n    - XOR:               `101000010110000101011` = 1316331\n- `1316331 & 0xFFFF` is `1316331 % 65536 = 1316331 - 20*65536 = 1316331 - 1310720 = 5611`\n- `x = 5611`\n\n---\n\n**Iteration 4:**\n- `i = 4`, `ch = 's'`\n- `ord('s')` is 115\n- `x = ((5611 << 5) ^ (5611 >> 3) ^ (115 * (4 + 1))) & 0xFFFF`\n- `5611 << 5` is `5611 * 32 = 179552`\n- `5611 >> 3` is `5611 // 8 = 701`\n- `115 * 5 = 575`\n- `179552 ^ 701 ^ 575`\n  - `179552 ^ 701`:\n    - 179552 in binary: `101100001001100000`\n    - 701 in binary:     `00000010101111101`\n    - XOR:              `101100100010011001` = 180089\n  - `180089 ^ 575`:\n    - 180089 in binary: `101100001001011001`\n    - 575 in binary:     `00000010001111111`\n    - XOR:              `101100101010100110` = 180646\n- `180646 & 0xFFFF` is `180646 % 65536 = 180646 - 2*65536 = 180646 - 131072 = 49574`\n- `x = 49574`\n\n---\n\nAfter all iterations, the program prints `49574`.\n\nANSWER: 49574"}
{"run_id": "full-r1", "item_id": "reasoning-009", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Round-robin scheduler with arrivals", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_high", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning high)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 22695, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 4189, "reasoning_tokens": 3804, "input_tokens": 251, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 832, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 10917, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "b:9,d:11,a:16,c:18,e:20", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "b:9,d:11,a:16,c:18,e:20"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj2a08ecae-d028-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj279088d8-d028-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T22:57:38-0500", "answer": "Tracing the round-robin schedule:\n\n- `clock=0`: admit `a`. Run `a` for 2 \u2192 `clock=2`, `a` remaining 3. Admit `b`, `c`. Queue: `b, c, a`.\n- Run `b` for 2 \u2192 `clock=4`, `b` remaining 1. Queue: `c, a, b`.\n- Run `c` for 2 \u2192 `clock=6`, `c` remaining 4. Admit `d`. Queue: `a, b, d, c`.\n- Run `a` for 2 \u2192 `clock=8`, `a` remaining 1. Admit `e`. Queue: `b, d, c, e, a`.\n- Run `b` for 1 \u2192 `clock=9`, `b` done. Queue: `d, c, e, a`.\n- Run `d` for 2 \u2192 `clock=11`, `d` done. Queue: `c, e, a`.\n- Run `c` for 2 \u2192 `clock=13`, `c` remaining 2. Queue: `e, a, c`.\n- Run `e` for 2 \u2192 `clock=15`, `e` remaining 2. Queue: `a, c, e`.\n- Run `a` for 1 \u2192 `clock=16`, `a` done. Queue: `c, e`.\n- Run `c` for 2 \u2192 `clock=18`, `c` done. Queue: `e`.\n- Run `e` for 2 \u2192 `clock=20`, `e` done.\n\nSo the completion order is `b, d, a, c, e`.\n\nANSWER: b:9,d:11,a:16,c:18,e:20"}
{"run_id": "full-r1", "item_id": "reasoning-013", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Most jobs finished by their deadlines", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l3", "config_label": "Mistral Large 3 (baseline)", "model": "mistral-large-2512", "reasoning_effort": null, "pass": false, "score": 0.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 28153, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 3112, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 190, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 8727, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 0.0, "pass": false, "expected": "6", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "5"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj29c9817b-d028-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj279088d7-d028-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T22:57:43-0500", "answer": "Alright, let's tackle this scheduling problem step by step. We have eight jobs, each with a processing time and a deadline. Our goal is to schedule these jobs on a single machine (which can handle only one job at a time) starting at time 0, such that the maximum number of jobs finish on or before their deadlines.\n\n**Understanding the Problem:**\n- **On-time job:** Finishes at or before its deadline.\n- **Late job:** Finishes after its deadline but still runs after all on-time jobs.\n- We can choose the order of jobs to maximize the number of on-time jobs.\n\n**Approach:**\nThis is a classic scheduling problem where we want to maximize the number of jobs completed on time. A common strategy for such problems is to use the **Earliest Deadline First (EDF)** algorithm, where we schedule jobs in order of their deadlines, earliest first. This is because scheduling jobs with earlier deadlines first tends to allow more jobs to meet their deadlines.\n\nHowever, we need to ensure that by scheduling a job, we don't make subsequent jobs late. So, we'll proceed by:\n1. Sorting the jobs based on their deadlines (earliest first).\n2. Sequentially adding jobs to the schedule, checking at each step if adding the next job would cause any already scheduled job to miss its deadline.\n3. If adding a job doesn't cause any already scheduled jobs to be late, we keep it; otherwise, we skip it.\n\nBut since we're trying to maximize the number of on-time jobs, we might need to consider that sometimes skipping a job with a very tight deadline could allow more jobs with slightly later deadlines to be on time.\n\nAn alternative approach is to consider all possible subsets of jobs and see which subset can be scheduled on time, but that's computationally expensive (2^8 = 256 subsets). Instead, we can use a greedy approach where we try to schedule as many jobs as possible by always picking the next job that can fit without making any already scheduled job late.\n\n**Given Jobs:**\nLet's list the jobs with their processing times and deadlines:\n\n| Job | Processing Time (p) | Deadline (d) |\n|-----|----------------------|--------------|\n| J1  | 4                    | 6            |\n| J2  | 2                    | 5            |\n| J3  | 5                    | 14           |\n| J4  | 3                    | 9            |\n| J5  | 6                    | 18           |\n| J6  | 2                    | 11           |\n| J7  | 4                    | 20           |\n| J8  | 3                    | 13           |\n\n**Sorting by Deadline:**\nFirst, let's sort the jobs by their deadlines (earliest first):\n\n1. J2: p=2, d=5\n2. J1: p=4, d=6\n3. J4: p=3, d=9\n4. J6: p=2, d=11\n5. J8: p=3, d=13\n6. J3: p=5, d=14\n7. J5: p=6, d=18\n8. J7: p=4, d=20\n\n**Greedy Scheduling:**\nNow, let's try to schedule them in this order, keeping track of the current time.\n\nInitialize current time = 0.\n\n1. Schedule J2:\n   - Starts at 0, finishes at 0 + 2 = 2.\n   - Deadline is 5. 2 \u2264 5 \u2192 on time.\n   - Current time = 2.\n\n2. Schedule J1:\n   - Starts at 2, finishes at 2 + 4 = 6.\n   - Deadline is 6. 6 \u2264 6 \u2192 on time.\n   - Current time = 6.\n\n3. Schedule J4:\n   - Starts at 6, finishes at 6 + 3 = 9.\n   - Deadline is 9. 9 \u2264 9 \u2192 on time.\n   - Current time = 9.\n\n4. Schedule J6:\n   - Starts at 9, finishes at 9 + 2 = 11.\n   - Deadline is 11. 11 \u2264 11 \u2192 on time.\n   - Current time = 11.\n\n5. Schedule J8:\n   - Starts at 11, finishes at 11 + 3 = 14.\n   - Deadline is 13. 14 > 13 \u2192 late.\n   - So, we cannot schedule J8 on time if we've already scheduled J6.\n   - Let's see if skipping J6 allows J8 to be on time.\n\n**Alternative Path: Skip J6, Schedule J8:**\nCurrent time = 9 (after J4).\n\n1. Skip J6.\n2. Schedule J8:\n   - Starts at 9, finishes at 9 + 3 = 12.\n   - Deadline is 13. 12 \u2264 13 \u2192 on time.\n   - Current time = 12.\n\nNow, can we schedule J6 after J8?\n- J6: p=2, d=11.\n- Starts at 12, finishes at 14.\n- Deadline is 11. 14 > 11 \u2192 late.\nSo, scheduling J8 prevents J6 from being on time. But we're trying to maximize the number of on-time jobs, and currently, we "}
{"run_id": "full-r1", "item_id": "reasoning-014", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Upgrade budget with dependencies", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l3", "config_label": "Mistral Large 3 (baseline)", "model": "mistral-large-2512", "reasoning_effort": null, "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 32670, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 3636, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 188, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 9039, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "142", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "142"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj2a384915-d028-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj2790afea-d028-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T22:57:48-0500", "answer": "Alright, let's tackle this problem step by step. We have a budget of 100 credits and a list of upgrades, each with a cost and a benefit. Some upgrades have additional constraints:\n\n1. **U4 can only be bought if U3 is also bought.**\n2. **U2 and U8 cannot both be bought.**\n\nOur goal is to select a combination of upgrades that maximizes the total benefit without exceeding the 100-credit budget, while respecting the given constraints.\n\n### Understanding the Upgrades\n\nFirst, let's list out all the upgrades with their costs and benefits:\n\n| Upgrade | Cost | Benefit |\n|---------|------|---------|\n| U1      | 28   | 40      |\n| U2      | 35   | 52      |\n| U3      | 17   | 22      |\n| U4      | 40   | 61      |\n| U5      | 12   | 14      |\n| U6      | 23   | 33      |\n| U7      | 9    | 10      |\n| U8      | 31   | 45      |\n\n### Constraints Recap\n\n1. **U4 requires U3**: If we choose U4, we must also choose U3.\n2. **U2 and U8 are mutually exclusive**: We can choose U2 or U8, but not both.\n\n### Approach to Solve the Problem\n\nThis is a classic **0/1 Knapsack problem** with additional constraints. The general approach is to consider all possible combinations of upgrades that fit within the budget and satisfy the constraints, then pick the one with the highest benefit.\n\nGiven the small number of upgrades (8), we can consider all possible combinations, but that would be 2^8 = 256 possibilities, which is manageable but time-consuming. Instead, we can use a more systematic approach by considering the constraints to reduce the number of combinations we need to evaluate.\n\n### Step-by-Step Selection\n\nLet's consider the constraints one by one.\n\n#### Constraint 1: U4 requires U3\n\nThis means that U4 cannot be selected alone; it must be paired with U3. So, any combination that includes U4 must also include U3.\n\n#### Constraint 2: U2 and U8 cannot both be selected\n\nThis means in any combination, we can have:\n- Neither U2 nor U8\n- Only U2\n- Only U8\n\nBut not both U2 and U8 together.\n\n### Strategy\n\nTo maximize the benefit, we should prioritize upgrades with the highest benefit-to-cost ratio. This helps in getting the most \"bang for the buck.\"\n\nLet's calculate the benefit-to-cost ratio for each upgrade:\n\n| Upgrade | Benefit/Cost | Ratio  |\n|---------|--------------|--------|\n| U1      | 40/28        | ~1.43  |\n| U2      | 52/35        | ~1.49  |\n| U3      | 22/17        | ~1.29  |\n| U4      | 61/40        | ~1.525 |\n| U5      | 14/12        | ~1.17  |\n| U6      | 33/23        | ~1.43  |\n| U7      | 10/9         | ~1.11  |\n| U8      | 45/31        | ~1.45  |\n\nFrom the ratios, the order from highest to lowest is: U4, U2, U8, U1/U6, U3, U5, U7.\n\nThis suggests that U4, U2, and U8 are among the most efficient in terms of benefit per cost.\n\nHowever, we have constraints involving U4, U2, and U8, so we need to be careful.\n\n### Considering U4 and U3\n\nSince U4 has the highest ratio, let's see if we can include it.\n\n- U4 costs 40, requires U3 which costs 17. Total cost so far: 40 + 17 = 57.\n- Remaining budget: 100 - 57 = 43.\n\nNow, let's see what else we can add within the remaining 43 credits, keeping in mind that we can't have both U2 and U8.\n\n#### Option 1: Include U2 (cost 35, benefit 52)\n\n- U2 costs 35, so total cost: 57 + 35 = 92.\n- Remaining budget: 100 - 92 = 8.\n- Can we add anything with 8 credits? U7 costs 9 (too much), U5 costs 12 (too much), U7 is the only one close but can't.\n- Total benefit: 61 (U4) + 22 (U3) + 52 (U2) = 135.\n\n#### Option 2: Include U8 (cost 31, benefit 45)\n\n- U8 costs 31, so total cost: 57 + 31 = 88.\n- Remaining budget: 100 - 88 = 12.\n- Can add U5 (cost 12, benefit 14).\n- Total cost: 88 + 12 = 100.\n- Total benefit: 61 (U4) + 22 (U3) + 45 (U8) + 14 (U5) = 142.\n\nThis is better than the previous option (135).\n\n#### Option 3: Don't include U2 or U8\n\nAfter U4 and U3 (cost 57), remaining budget is 43.\n\nWhat can we add?\n\nPossible upgrades not yet considered: U1, U5, U6, U7.\n\nLet's try adding U1 (cost 28, benefit 40):\n\n- Cost: 57 + 28 = 85.\n"}
{"run_id": "full-r1", "item_id": "reasoning-013", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Most jobs finished by their deadlines", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_off", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning off)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "none", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 39443, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 6740, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 184, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 11829, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "6", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "6"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj29c9817c-d028-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj279088d7-d028-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T22:57:54-0500", "answer": "I need to find the maximum number of jobs that can finish on time, which is a classic scheduling problem. The optimal strategy is to use Earliest Deadline First (EDF) - schedule jobs in order of increasing deadlines, and count how many can finish on time.\n\nLet me list the jobs with processing times and deadlines:\n- J1: p=4, d=6\n- J2: p=2, d=5\n- J3: p=5, d=14\n- J4: p=3, d=9\n- J5: p=6, d=18\n- J6: p=2, d=11\n- J7: p=4, d=20\n- J8: p=3, d=13\n\nSort by deadline:\n- J2: p=2, d=5\n- J1: p=4, d=6\n- J4: p=3, d=9\n- J6: p=2, d=11\n- J8: p=3, d=13\n- J3: p=5, d=14\n- J5: p=6, d=18\n- J7: p=4, d=20\n\nNow let me check completion times:\n- J2: starts at 0, finishes at 2. Deadline 5. \u2713 On time (2 \u2264 5)\n- J1: starts at 2, finishes at 6. Deadline 6. \u2713 On time (6 \u2264 6)\n- J4: starts at 6, finishes at 9. Deadline 9. \u2713 On time (9 \u2264 9)\n- J6: starts at 9, finishes at 11. Deadline 11. \u2713 On time (11 \u2264 11)\n- J8: starts at 11, finishes at 14. Deadline 13. \u2717 Late (14 > 13)\n- J3: starts at 14, finishes at 19. Deadline 14. \u2717 Late (19 > 14)\n- J5: starts at 19, finishes at 25. Deadline 18. \u2717 Late (25 > 18)\n- J7: starts at 25, finishes at 29. Deadline 20. \u2717 Late (29 > 20)\n\nWith EDF order, I get 4 on-time jobs. But let me verify if we can do better by trying different combinations.\n\nActually, let me check if we can get 5 on time. The total processing time of all jobs is 4+2+5+3+6+2+4+3 = 29 hours.\n\nFor 5 jobs to be on time, I need to find 5 jobs where there's a schedule meeting all deadlines.\n\nLet me try to find the best 5 jobs. Looking at tight deadlines: J2 (d=5), J1 (d=6), J4 (d=9), J6 (d=11), J8 (d=13).\n\nTry J2, J1, J4, J6, J8: total time = 2+4+3+2+3 = 14. Need to fit in deadlines.\n- J2 first: finish 2 \u2264 5 \u2713\n- J1: finish 6 \u2264 6 \u2713\n- J4: finish 9 \u2264 9 \u2713\n- J6: finish 11 \u2264 11 \u2713\n- J8: finish 14 > 13 \u2717\n\nTry different order for these 5? By EDF it's already optimal for feasibility. J8 misses.\n\nTry J2, J1, J4, J6, J3: times 2,4,3,2,5 = 16. Deadlines 5,6,9,11,14.\n- J2: 2 \u2264 5 \u2713\n- J1: 6 \u2264 6 \u2713\n- J4: 9 \u2264 9 \u2713\n- J6: 11 \u2264 11 \u2713\n- J3: 16 > 14 \u2717\n\nTry J2, J1, J4, J8, J3: times 2,4,3,3,5 = 17. Deadlines 5,6,9,13,14.\n- J2: 2 \u2264 5 \u2713\n- J1: 6 \u2264 6 \u2713\n- J4: 9 \u2264 9 \u2713\n- J8: 12 \u2264 13 \u2713\n- J3: 17 > 14 \u2717\n\nTry J2, J1, J6, J8, J3: times 2,4,2,3,5 = 16. Deadlines 5,6,11,13,14.\n- J2: 2 \u2264 5 \u2713\n- J1: 6 \u2264 6 \u2713\n- J6: 8 \u2264 11 \u2713\n- J8: 11 \u2264 13 \u2713\n- J3: 16 > 14 \u2717\n\nTry J2, J4, J6, J8, J3: times 2,3,2,3,5 = 15. Deadlines 5,9,11,13,14.\n- J2: 2 \u2264 5 \u2713\n- J4: 5 \u2264 9 \u2713\n- J6: 7 \u2264 11 \u2713\n- J8: 10 \u2264 13 \u2713\n- J3: 15 > 14 \u2717\n\nTry J1, J4, J6, J8, J3: times 4,3,2,3,5 = 17. Deadlines 6,9,11,13,14.\n- J1: 4 \u2264 6 \u2713\n- J4: 7 \u2264 9 \u2713\n- J6: 9 \u2264 11 \u2713\n- J8: 12 \u2264 13 \u2713\n- J3: 17 > 14 \u2717\n\nTry J2, J1, J4, J6, J5? J5 has deadline 18, time 6. Total = 2+4+3+2+6 = 17.\n- J2: 2 \u2264 5 \u2713\n- J1: 6 \u2264 6 \u2713\n- J4: 9 \u2264 9 \u2713\n- J6: 11 \u2264 11 \u2713\n- J5: 17 \u2264 18 \u2713\n\nThat's 5 jobs on time! Let me verify: J2, J1, J4, J6, J5 in that order.\n- J2: 0 to 2, deadline 5 \u2713\n- J1: 2 to 6, deadline 6 \u2713\n- J4: 6 to 9, deadline 9 \u2713\n- J6: 9 to 11, deadline 11 \u2713\n- J5: 11 to 17, deadline 18 \u2713\n\nYes! 5 jobs on time.\n\nCan we get 6? Let's try adding another job. The remaining jobs are J3 (5,14), J7 (4,20), J8 (3,13).\n\nTry J2, J1, J4, J6, J8, J5: times 2,4,3,2,3,6 = 20. Deadlines 5,6,9,11,13,18.\n- J2: 2 \u2264 5 \u2713\n- J1: 6 \u2264 6 \u2713\n- J4: 9 \u2264 9 \u2713\n- J6: 11 \u2264 11 \u2713\n- J8: 14 > 13 \u2717\n\nTry J2, J1, J4, J6, J5, J7: times 2,4,3,2,6,4 = 21. Deadlines 5,6,9,11,18,20.\n- J2: 2 \u2264 5 \u2713\n- J1: 6 \u2264 6 \u2713\n- J4: 9 \u2264 9 \u2713\n- J6: 11 \u2264 11 \u2713\n- J5: 17 \u2264 18 \u2713\n- J7: 21 > 20 \u2717\n\nTry J2, J1, J4, J8, J5, J7: times 2,4,3,3,6,4 = 22. Deadlines 5,6,9,13,18,20.\n- J2: 2 \u2264 5 \u2713\n- J1: 6 \u2264 6 \u2713\n- J4: 9 \u2264 9 \u2713\n- J8: 12 \u2264 13 \u2713\n- J5: 18 \u2264 18 \u2713\n- J7: 22 > 20 \u2717\n\nTry J2, J1, J6, J8, J5, J7: times 2,4,2,3,6,4 = 21. Deadlines 5,6,11,13,18,20.\n- J2: 2 \u2264 5 \u2713\n- J1: 6 \u2264 6 \u2713\n- J6: 8 \u2264 11 \u2713\n- J8: 11 \u2264 13 \u2713\n- J5: 17 \u2264 18 \u2713\n- J7: 21 > 20 \u2717\n\nTry J2, J4, J6, J8, J5, J7: times 2,3,2,3,6,4 = 20. Deadlines 5,9,11,13,18,20.\n- J2: 2 \u2264 5 \u2713\n- J4: 5 \u2264 9 \u2713\n- J6: 7 \u2264 11 \u2713\n- J8: 10 \u2264 13 \u2713\n- J5: 16 \u2264 18 \u2713\n- J7: 20 \u2264 20 \u2713\n\nThat's 6 jobs on time! Let me verify: J2, J4, J6, J8, J5, J7.\n- J2: 0 to 2, deadl"}
{"run_id": "full-r1", "item_id": "reasoning-016", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Four houses: owners and pets", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_high", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning high)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 40254, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 6682, "reasoning_tokens": 6374, "input_tokens": 237, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 1026, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 22801, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "Leo, Jon, Ivy, Kim, fish, bird, cat, dog", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "Leo, Jon, Ivy, Kim, fish, bird, cat, dog"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj2a270ae6-d028-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj2790afe9-d028-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T22:57:56-0500", "answer": "Jon must be in house 2 or 3 because the fish is immediately to his left and the blue house is immediately to his right.\n\n- If Jon were in house 3, then Leo would be in house 4, but the red house must be to the right of Leo\u2019s house, impossible.\n- So Jon is in house 2, the fish is in house 1, and the blue house is in house 3.\n\nThen Ivy and Kim must be next door, so they occupy houses 3 and 4, leaving Leo in house 1.\n\nThe dog must be at least two houses away from Jon (house 2), so the dog is in house 4.\n\nKim cannot be in house 3, because then the green house would have to be house 1, making Leo live in the green house, which is forbidden. So Kim is in house 4 and Ivy is in house 3.\n\nThen the green house must be house 2, the red house must be house 4, and house 1 is white. The bird is next door to Ivy (house 3), so the bird is in house 2, leaving the cat in house 3.\n\nFinal arrangement:\n- House 1: Leo, fish\n- House 2: Jon, bird\n- House 3: Ivy, cat\n- House 4: Kim, dog\n\nANSWER: Leo, Jon, Ivy, Kim, fish, bird, cat, dog"}
